{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"abf2c37158aa7468","type":"text","text":"**SPACES**\n\nDiscovery: Odyssey search.\n\nParticipation: Discussion forum.\n\nImprovement: Critique jury.\n\nProof: Showcasing.\n\nKnowledge base: Resource.","x":-1700,"y":-1440,"width":300,"height":260},
		{"id":"fc9d626fe2d1be11","type":"text","text":"**ODYSSEY**\nI would feel the missing piece when the product immediately did four things:\n\nIt told me my studio.\n\nIt showed me one good example of how people post here.\n\nIt let me publish with guidance, not fear.\n\nIt showed my contribution becoming part of a visible improvement loop.\n\nThat is the moment when the platform stops feeling like “a place with features” and starts feeling like “a community with memory”","x":-2720,"y":-1487,"width":672,"height":354,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"88d5ec00581818e6","type":"text","text":"A Discussion thread that turns into \"I want feedback on my actual work\" can have a clear \"Move to Critique\" action, starting a new structured critique thread pre-filled with context from the discussion.","x":-660,"y":-1080,"width":250,"height":269},
		{"id":"8084c0fc045d2c36","type":"text","text":"**DISCUSSION**\nDiscussion becomes the \"town square\" — the place where all kinds of interaction happen, ideas surface, questions get asked, and community identity forms","x":-838,"y":-1378,"width":428,"height":151,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"90f3187ffb9c53a4","type":"text","text":"**CRITIQUE**\nCritique needs strict structural discipline to work: context framing, staged feedback (concept → wireframe → polish), qualifiers instead of opinions, and documented decisions","x":-1640,"y":-800,"width":802,"height":100,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"cbbf2ca465ab34af","type":"text","text":"Critique threads can surface a lightweight discussion link-back (\"this critique sparked a broader discussion\") so insights don't get siloed, but the critique record itself stays clean and immutable as documentation","x":-660,"y":-700,"width":380,"height":149},
		{"id":"2a3841a5a0ed9f32","type":"text","text":"Discussion's job simplifies to being genuinely welcoming and broad: quick questions, deeper knowledge threads, opinions, career talk, and community energy — the things that don't need staged facilitation but do need good structure (categories, tags, canonical answers) to stay clean. This actually strengthens your earlier goal of Discussion feeling like a \"unique Q&A/knowledge space\" rather than chaotic — because it's no longer carrying the weight of formal feedback, it can stay lighter, faster, and more approachable for beginners, while Critique becomes the serious, hierarchical space for tracked growth.","x":54,"y":-836,"width":530,"height":285},
		{"id":"16f76f6f3759bbcc","type":"file","file":"Pasted image 20260726132830.png","x":-40,"y":-1567,"width":624,"height":340},
		{"id":"4342cb16b2a3761a","type":"file","file":"WhatsApp Image 2026-07-26 at 12.47.27.jpeg","x":-824,"y":-2358,"width":784,"height":568},
		{"id":"0d51c3eefd0f2296","type":"text","text":"Stanford research on online trust found that people trust strangers faster online when reputation and rating systems are visible, but that trust can flatten into transactional behavior if not paired with genuine interpersonal warmth. For Atelier, this means reputation/craft-tags should build credibility, but human touches (thank-yous, mentorship, follow-up replies) should build the warmth that keeps people emotionally invested, not just transactionally satisfied.","x":200,"y":-2266,"width":627,"height":192},
		{"id":"ef1806989be7ae7b","type":"text","text":"**Cohort Growth**\nWhen multiple users post critiques in the same Studio within a similar timeframe on similar topics (e.g., \"brand identity,\" \"residential massing\"), group them loosely into a Crit Week — not a rigid cohort, but a discoverable cluster. \"4 logo critiques this week in Graphic Design — see how the community is growing.\" This is lightweight but creates the feeling of shared progress.","x":-1640,"y":-261,"width":681,"height":162},
		{"id":"747776ef2ae2af82","type":"text","text":"Iteration tracker (v1 → v2 → v3 with change notes) is genuinely powerful\navi1-1.md\n\nSelf-analysis pre-critique questions teach beginners to think before asking\nAtelier-Principles.md\n\nThe \"Feedback requested\" callout gives critics direction — critical for quality","x":-894,"y":-261,"width":614,"height":219},
		{"id":"fc31a836e9b497f8","type":"file","file":"Critique framwork V1.md","x":-1774,"y":109,"width":950,"height":291},
		{"id":"21938682afda4098","type":"file","file":"Critique V1.md","x":-1774,"y":560,"width":905,"height":400},
		{"id":"1c3396ca21887b58","type":"text","text":"**SHOWCASE**\nShowcase is the finished-work gallery — not where critique happens, but where a piece \"graduates\" after iteration and becomes proof of skill. It should feel like a curated exhibition, not a feed, reinforcing \"Structured over Chaotic\" [space-instructions]. Every published Showcase piece should visibly connect back to the creator's profile, since the profile itself is meant to read like a self-explanatory portfolio of ideology and workflow, not just a resume.","x":-2145,"y":-2320,"width":505,"height":276,"color":"#96c318"},
		{"id":"55ec242f7e9d895f","type":"text","text":"Since you want the profile to speak for itself without explanation, Showcase pieces should each carry a compact \"signature strip\" — avatar, Honor badge, discipline, and a shareable Honor Card link. This means anyone landing on a single project can click straight into the full profile and immediately grasp the person's trajectory: Studios joined, problems solved, critiques given, other Showcase work — all without the creator writing an \"About Me\" essay. The profile becomes the implicit case-study","x":-3065,"y":-2248,"width":805,"height":204},
		{"id":"c4a1796af50d560d","type":"text","text":"**What to Deliberately Avoid**\nNo follower counts or public leaderboards on Showcase itself — that drifts toward social-media vanity metrics, which the principles explicitly reject.\nAtelier-Principles.md\n\nNo infinite auto-play carousel or algorithmic feed ranking by engagement alone — favor curated/staff-pick rotation to protect quality over virality.\nAtelier-Principles.md\n\nNo forcing detailed case-study write-ups for every post — keep the default upload light (image + one-line intent); deeper storytelling (iteration history, process notes) should be optional and progressive, not a barrier to publishing.\n\nThis keeps Showcase simple enough for a first-time student to post confidently, while still being the space where a five-year veteran's work looks unmistakably premium and earns them real platform-wide recognition.","x":-2384,"y":-2840,"width":785,"height":324},
		{"id":"9fd5f1789c283375","type":"text","text":"| Section                | Definition for Odyssey                                                                                                                                                                    | Key design choices                                                                                                                                                                                                | Links to maturity model                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Purpose                | Search + discovery + creative matchmaking space for a design community. Helps users find specific answers, explore posts/projects/people, and spark cross‑discipline ideas. uxdesign+1    | Combine strong search UX (predictive, filtered, relevant) with an Explore layer that surfaces trending, personalized, and cross‑domain content. designrush+1                                                      | All stages: Direct, Adjacent, Unexpected share this core purpose but expose it at different depths. nngroup+1                                                                                                          |\n| User types             | Beginners, focused practitioners, cross‑domain explorers, mentors/senior designers. carriemelissajones+1                                                                                  | Map UX to user intent: beginners get clarity and safety; explorers get breadth; mentors get ways to find people and threads to help. carriemelissajones+1                                                         | Beginners mostly see Direct; practitioners reach Adjacent; explorers/mentors unlock more Unexpected content. userpilot+1                                                                                               |\n| Entry states           | Global search bar, Odyssey Explore page, onboarding flow, deep links (notifications, shares), Studio-scoped entry. support.patreon+2                                                      | Preserve context (Studio, interests) but always allow broadening. Search is visible everywhere; Explore is a dedicated destination. designrush+1                                                                  | New users mainly enter via onboarding and Explore; returning users use search, Explore, and deep links tied to their current stage. productquantyoutube                                                                |\n| First-time journey     | Short orientation → interest selection → one quick win → light Explore → simple action (save/follow/search). pendo+2                                                                      | Focus on “time to first value”: show one relevant solved post/resource + one curated collection + one designer to follow. Avoid feature tours. appcues+1                                                          | First visit is firmly in Direct layer, with a small glimpse of Adjacent; Unexpected is intentionally hidden until later sessions. nngroup+1                                                                            |\n| Discovery layers (PDL) | 3 progressive layers: Direct → Adjacent → Unexpected. nngroup+1                                                                                                                           | Direct: precise, relevant results and simple Explore. Adjacent: related topics/people across Studios. Unexpected: cross‑domain inspirations with strong conceptual links. journals.sagepub+2                      | This is the maturity model: Stage 1 = Direct; Stage 2 = Adjacent; Stage 3 = Unexpected, governed by usage and engagement signals. ixdf+1                                                                               |\n| Core components        | Global search, search results page, Explore home, People discovery, Collections/saved items, signals/analytics layer. designrush+2                                                        | Search: predictive, faceted, helpful empty states. Explore: “For You”, “Trending in your Studios”, “New designers”, “Cross‑domain inspiration”. Collections: curated learning paths/reference boards. getstream+2 | Components gradually reveal more capabilities: beginners mostly use search + basic Explore; advanced users see richer People, Collections, and cross‑domain sections. nngroup+1                                        |\n| Content types          | Threads (questions/discussions), solved issues, resources, examples/projects, designers/mentors, collections/playlists. marvelapp+2                                                       | Result tabs by type; clear badges (Solved, Resource, Project, Person, Collection); keyword highlighting and Studio labels for scanability. baymard+1                                                              | Direct layer emphasizes solved issues and resources; Adjacent adds related threads and people; Unexpected introduces cross‑Studio examples and collections. journals.sagepub+1                                         |\n| Actions & permissions  | Search, filter, open, save/bookmark, follow designers/topics, join Studios, share links, react/comment. Moderators/curators can pin, promote, and de‑emphasize content. support.patreon+1 | Keep actions simple and consistent: every card supports “Open”, “Save”, “Follow/Join”. Curators can mark exemplar content and build featured collections. tspa+1                                                  | As users mature, they gain more meaningful actions (e.g., creating public collections, curating cross‑domain lists), but the core actions stay familiar. nngroup+1                                                     |\n| Moderation logic       | Focus on discovery quality: quality thresholds, Studio relevance, spam/abuse filters, curator lanes. tspa+1                                                                               | Only content with minimum positive signals or endorsements can appear in high‑visibility discovery slots; cross‑domain suggestions must pass relevance rules. link.springer+1                                     | Higher maturity stages can show more experimental content, but quality and relevance gates prevent random or harmful suggestions. acm+1                                                                                |\n| Success metrics        | Activation, search success, explore engagement, maturity signals, retention. pragmaticinstitute+1                                                                                         | Examples: time to first useful result; search-to-click and solve rate; Explore-driven saves/follows; cross‑Studio engagement; adoption of adjacent/unexpected content. experienceleague.adobe+1                   | Use metrics per stage: Stage 1 (Direct) = fast answers; Stage 2 (Adjacent) = growth in related exploration; Stage 3 (Unexpected) = healthy cross‑domain engagement without confusion or drop‑off. pragmaticinstitute+1 |","x":-4140,"y":-1680,"width":1156,"height":1280},
		{"id":"d19bdc8b862d80cc","type":"text","text":"**Framework Metrics**\n\n- Purpose.\n\n- User types.\n\n- Entry states.\n\n- First-time journey.\n\n- Core components.\n\n- Content types.\n\n- Actions and permissions.\n\n- Progressive disclosure layers.\n\n- Moderation logic.\n\n- Success metrics.","x":-1220,"y":-2941,"width":250,"height":526},
		{"id":"d31ffdda6d9a6283","type":"file","file":"Showcase.md","x":-4140,"y":-2346,"width":646,"height":400},
		{"id":"985549e506d6d89c","type":"file","file":"Profile.md","x":-4140,"y":-6160,"width":1926,"height":1318},
		{"id":"82399b96598b58e0","type":"text","text":"**The Community Commitment Curve as the backbone** - Onboarding\n\nThe most proven framework for this is the Community Commitment Curve: discover → join → onboard → engage → lead, where each stage asks slightly more of the member than the last. For Atelier, onboarding should map to this curve explicitly — a new user's very first ask should be nearly effortless (choose your discipline, browse examples), while heavier asks (post a question, submit for critique) come only after trust and orientation are established. Architecting steps from easy to hard is critical because early over-asking (e.g., \"introduce yourself to 500 strangers\") is one of the top reasons onboarding fails and people churn silently.\nguild\n+2\n\nStage-by-stage flow for Atelier\n1. Orientation, not a tour. Instead of a generic feature walkthrough, the first screen should answer \"what is this place for\" and \"who is like me here,\" using a short, human explanation plus visible examples of real good questions and threads rather than empty placeholder screens. Community onboarding guides stress that new members need clarity on what to expect, the norms/rituals of the space, and how to make their first contribution — this should be visual and quick, not a wall of text.\nnoeleflowers\n+3\n\n2. Identity and interest capture (a welcome survey, not a form). Ask 2-3 lightweight questions — discipline (UI, illustration, architecture, etc.), experience level, and what they're hoping to get out of the space — framed conversationally rather than as a signup form. This personalizes everything downstream (which threads are surfaced, which category is suggested first) and signals that Atelier already understands the difference between a beginner and a senior professional.\nchameleon\n+2\n\n3. A safe first action with a guaranteed quick win. The very first ask should never be \"post something publicly.\" Effective onboarding gives early quick wins — like reacting to a thread, saving a resource, or following one topic — so the user feels recognized and successful before any exposure risk. Gamified progress (a checklist, a small badge for completing setup) reinforces this without feeling like busywork, as long as it's tied to meaningful steps rather than arbitrary points.\nzigpoll\n+2\n\n4. Guided first contribution, heavily scaffolded. Only after orientation and a quick win should the flow nudge toward asking a first question or replying to someone else's — using a structured prompt (\"describe your context, what you tried, what you need\") rather than a blank box, since structured prompts measurably reduce the fear of \"asking it wrong.\" Real-time feedback immediately after this action (a confirmation, a fast first response, a visible \"someone is looking at this\") is essential, because silence after a first contribution is one of the fastest ways to lose a new member's trust.\nappcues\n+3\n\n5. Return-trigger touchpoints. Since most new members will not complete onboarding in a single session, the flow should continue asynchronously through targeted nudges (an email or in-app prompt tied to their specific next step, not generic marketing) so momentum isn't lost. Chameleon's onboarding-flow research emphasizes designing around one activation milestone at a time and measuring activation rate, not just completion of steps, so Atelier's flow should track \"did they get real value\" rather than \"did they click through everything.\"\nchameleon\n+3\n\n","x":-4140,"y":-4720,"width":1926,"height":1200,"color":"#063715"},
		{"id":"21792a4055176310","type":"file","file":"Research/Framework Implementation Strategy Phases.md","x":-6160,"y":-5042,"width":1320,"height":400},
		{"id":"c1b07c026bce176a","type":"text","text":"**RESOURCES**\nThink of the Resource Section as a “living learning archive” that sits alongside Studios, discussions, and critiques, instead of a standalone folder of bookmarks.\n\n- Its purpose is to give members discipline-specific handbooks, standards, and case studies that directly support asking better questions, giving better critique, and improving work.\n    \n- It should be curated enough that beginners feel “this is where I find what actually helps my project,”","x":-3882,"y":-99,"width":816,"height":264,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"0d66b08c00b08524","type":"file","file":"Resources.md","x":-5502,"y":84,"width":612,"height":400},
		{"id":"f556c3ddf2dd7b17","type":"text","text":"Concrete ways to integrate it into the journey:\n\nContextual surfacing:\n\nShow relevant resources inside a discussion or critique (e.g., “Recommended: Basic typography grid guide” when someone posts a layout issue).\n\nAttach resources to “Resolved” discussions so future learners see both the conversation and the supporting material side by side.\n\nPer-discussion suggestions:\n\nWhen someone is creating a post (“I’m stuck with my portfolio layout”), suggest 1–3 resources based on tags they select (Studio, topic, stage).\n\nPersonal archive in the profile:\n\nShow “Resources I’ve used/saved” as part of the profile, next to “Discussions I’ve started” and “Critiques I’ve given,” so it feels like part of their creative identity and learning path.","x":-3882,"y":324,"width":898,"height":444}
	],
